1
00:00:00,585 --> 00:00:03,028
Fine-grained access control is a solved problem.

2
00:00:03,028 --> 00:00:05,828
A token can be limited to one project, or to read-only.

3
00:00:05,828 --> 00:00:09,646
What is much harder is temporary delegation at the moment the work happens:

4
00:00:09,646 --> 00:00:13,311
a person deciding, right now, that an assistant may touch these records,

5
00:00:13,311 --> 00:00:15,703
through these fields, for the next ten minutes.

6
00:00:15,703 --> 00:00:17,383
This CRM is the host application.

7
00:00:17,383 --> 00:00:21,608
The layer underneath turns that decision into a permission an agent can be held to.

8
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:23,908
Here I select two customer records.

9
00:00:23,908 --> 00:00:28,184
Nothing has been delegated — selecting something in the interface does not change

10
00:00:28,184 --> 00:00:29,715
what an assistant may access.

11
00:00:29,715 --> 00:00:32,144
It only defines the scope I am about to offer.

12
00:00:32,595 --> 00:00:33,348
Now I grant it:

13
00:00:33,348 --> 00:00:36,310
these two accounts, only these two fields, for ten minutes,

14
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:38,317
with the countdown starting immediately.

15
00:00:38,317 --> 00:00:39,221
That is a mandate.

16
00:00:39,671 --> 00:00:42,059
The moment I approve it, three things happen.

17
00:00:42,059 --> 00:00:43,810
The delegated records are marked.

18
00:00:43,810 --> 00:00:47,737
The panel reads the exact scope back to me, so I can check what I allowed.

19
00:00:47,737 --> 00:00:50,603
And the permission is compiled into a live WebMCP tool

20
00:00:50,603 --> 00:00:54,795
— one that did not exist before the grant, exposing only the records and fields

21
00:00:54,795 --> 00:00:55,856
this mandate covers.

22
00:00:56,307 --> 00:00:57,812
The assistant can now call it.

23
00:00:57,812 --> 00:01:01,725
Its first request is inside the mandate, so the server accepts it and stages a

24
00:01:01,725 --> 00:01:04,485
change against the record — visible, but not committed.

25
00:01:04,485 --> 00:01:08,348
Then it asks for a record that was never delegated, and the call is rejected.

26
00:01:08,348 --> 00:01:11,057
Enforcement does not rest on what the tool advertises:

27
00:01:11,057 --> 00:01:13,616
every call reaches the server carrying its mandate,

28
00:01:13,616 --> 00:01:16,375
and the scope is checked again before anything happens.

29
00:01:16,827 --> 00:01:18,196
There is a second boundary.

30
00:01:18,196 --> 00:01:21,035
The assistant can prepare a change but cannot commit one

31
00:01:21,035 --> 00:01:23,621
— no apply tool is registered for the agent at all,

32
00:01:23,621 --> 00:01:25,954
and the inspector makes that absence explicit.

33
00:01:25,954 --> 00:01:29,300
The final action stays with the person who granted the permission.

34
00:01:29,752 --> 00:01:34,113
This panel exists only to explain what is happening; a real user needs none of it.

35
00:01:34,113 --> 00:01:37,890
The production interface is a persistent badge while a mandate is live,

36
00:01:37,890 --> 00:01:41,027
and an indication on each record of what the agent proposed

37
00:01:41,027 --> 00:01:43,740
— a running delegation must never become invisible.

38
00:01:44,191 --> 00:01:46,724
The mechanism is not specific to CRM software.

39
00:01:46,724 --> 00:01:49,531
This is the same layer inside a deployment console:

40
00:01:49,531 --> 00:01:54,101
the resources are services, the permitted properties are replica counts and feature

41
00:01:54,101 --> 00:01:54,431
flags.

42
00:01:54,431 --> 00:01:58,670
The generated tool changes because it is derived from what the host declares.

43
00:01:58,670 --> 00:02:00,322
The delegation model does not.

44
00:02:00,774 --> 00:02:03,923
So far this has been the mechanism from inside the application.

45
00:02:03,923 --> 00:02:05,573
Here it is from the agent's side:

46
00:02:05,573 --> 00:02:08,322
ChatGPT Desktop, using this site's actual WebMCP tools,

47
00:02:08,322 --> 00:02:11,521
making real calls against the permission I granted a minute ago.

48
00:02:11,521 --> 00:02:13,970
Which is how delegation works between people too.

49
00:02:13,970 --> 00:02:16,819
If a colleague covers for me I don't hand over my account

50
00:02:16,819 --> 00:02:20,318
— I tell them what they may handle, how far it goes, and when it ends.

51
00:02:20,318 --> 00:02:21,718
The decision already exists.

52
00:02:21,718 --> 00:02:24,917
A mandate is what turns it into something a machine can enforce.

53
00:02:25,274 --> 00:02:25,432
So:

54
00:02:25,432 --> 00:02:28,702
a person defines a temporary scope at the moment it is needed,

55
00:02:28,702 --> 00:02:31,867
the system compiles that intent into a live WebMCP contract,

56
00:02:31,867 --> 00:02:33,818
and re-validates it on every request.

57
00:02:33,818 --> 00:02:36,403
Today I granted that from inside the application.

58
00:02:36,403 --> 00:02:40,147
The next step is to move the decision into the agent's own conversation

59
00:02:40,147 --> 00:02:44,420
— it asks for the access it needs, the person approves a temporary mandate there,

60
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:46,793
and no broader credential ever changes hands.
